% T10 (2026-09-29 review remediation): registration that must keep refusing,
% templates that must keep their bytes, and the rendering questions a snapshot
% of one translation cannot see. Every runner translates each case a second
% time on the SAME bindings and program object and requires the same outcome
% (the "reuse twin", the way `--- as statement` has its translateStatement twin),
% so a case that refuses once and then silently accepts fails here without a
% case of its own -- which is exactly how a memoise-before-check looks.

### name: register.guard.refused-on-every-use-lacking-the-pattern
--- note
sql/MAP.md section 7, rule 10 at run time: a `numericGuard` that does not carry
the numeral pattern of the dialect's `ISNUM` raises the registration error every
time the guard is used. JS-C24, PHP-C49, PY-C49, CPP-C36 and LISP-C42 marked the
dialect as checked BEFORE checking it, so the first translation was refused and
every later one emitted the SQL the check had rejected. The runner's second
translation of this case is what asserts the refusal repeats.
--- dialect
pgbad
--- register
[{"dialect": "pgbad", "extends": "postgresql", "version": "16",
  "lexical": {"numericGuard": "CASE WHEN ({textCast:0} ~ '^.*$') THEN CAST({0} AS NUMERIC) ELSE NULL END"}}]
--- bindings
{"NAME": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
NAME + 1
--- throws
LogicException
===

### name: register.guard.refused-on-every-use-wrong-pattern
--- note
The same rule with a guard that has a pattern, but not the numeral grammar.
--- dialect
mdbad
--- register
[{"dialect": "mdbad", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5",
  "lexical": {"numericGuard": "CASE WHEN ({0} REGEXP 'x') THEN CAST({0} AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END"}}]
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "x", "type": "UNKNOWN"}}
--- source
X > 5
--- throws
LogicException
===

### name: register.guard.a-sound-guard-is-accepted-every-time
--- note
CONTROL for the two above: the shipped guard, registered as a derived dialect,
translates, and translates again (the runner's twin) to the same bytes.
--- dialect
pgok
--- register
[{"dialect": "pgok", "extends": "postgresql", "version": "16"}]
--- bindings
{"NAME": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
NAME + 1
--- expect
(CAST(CASE WHEN (CAST("o"."name" AS TEXT) ~ '^-?[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$') THEN CAST("o"."name" AS NUMERIC) ELSE NULL END AS NUMERIC) + CAST(1 AS NUMERIC))
===

### name: register.template.non-ascii-bytes-are-kept
--- note
GO-C24: Emit.Fill copied a template one BYTE at a time as a character, so every
byte of a multi-byte character above U+007F came out as a separate code point
(`Ã©`, `Å¼`). A template is UTF-8 text and is copied as such.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "F({0}, 'é ż ć 𝄞')", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- bindings
{"NAME": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
UPPER(NAME)
--- expect
F(`o`.`name`, 'é ż ć 𝄞')
===

### name: register.template.non-ascii-bytes-are-kept-in-a-literal-template
--- note
The same through a template that carries a literal of its own alongside a bound
argument, so the argument's own text (also non-ASCII) is not confused with it.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- register
[{"define": ["sqlite", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "MY_CRC('zażółć', {0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("źdźbło")
--- expect
MY_CRC('zażółć', 'źdźbło')
===

### name: register.template.empty-tail-list-emits-nothing
--- note
sql/MAP.md section 4 spells `{n:}` "arguments n onward, joined with `, `". With
no argument at n or beyond the join is over nothing and emits nothing; it is not
a refusal (GO-C39a: Go refused it as neither an argument nor a lexical entry).
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "F({0}, {1:})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- bindings
{"NAME": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
UPPER(NAME)
--- expect
F(`o`.`name`, )
===

### name: register.template.empty-lexical-value-emits-nothing
--- note
`null` withdraws a lexical key (sql/MAP.md section 3); the empty string is a
value, and it fills as nothing. GO-C39b: Go refused `valStr == ""` as if it
were withdrawn.
--- dialect
mdempty
--- register
[{"dialect": "mdempty", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textCollate": ""}},
 {"define": ["mdempty", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "F({0}{textCollate})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- bindings
{"NAME": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
UPPER(NAME)
--- expect
F(`o`.`name`)
===

### name: mode.params.order-follows-the-statement-not-the-source
--- note
Parameters are numbered in the order the placeholders APPEAR in the statement.
A LINK renders its ON condition before the WHERE that follows it, so the value
of the second stage is bound first: `params` is `yy, zz`, not the `zz, yy` of
the program's text. Every slot has a value and every value a slot (the runner
checks that for each case), so a statement rendered and thrown away shows here.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "alias": "r", "fields": {"A": {"column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, "N": {"column": "n", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "S": {"kind": "relation", "from": "s", "alias": "s", "fields": {"A": {"column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, "M": {"column": "m", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- mode
params
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> FILTER(_["N"] $== "zz") .> LINK(S, _1["A"] == _2["A"] AND _2["M"] $== "yy") .> MAP(RECORD("n", _["N"], "m", _["M"]))
--- expect
SELECT `r`.`n` AS `n`, `s`.`m` AS `m` FROM `r` `r` INNER JOIN `s` `s` ON ((`r`.`a` = `s`.`a`) AND (CAST(`s`.`m` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin)) WHERE (CAST(`r`.`n` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin)
--- params
t"yy", t"zz"
===

### name: mode.params.no-orphan-slot-through-sort-take-link
--- note
CPP-C57 and LISP-C40: a statement with a literal in a FILTER before a SORT, a TAKE and a
LINK is validated once as a prefix and discarded, and the slots of that pass
stayed in `params`: three values bound for two slots, so an application binding
`params` sends a parameter the statement has no place for. The runner asserts,
for every case, that every slot has a value and every value has a slot.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "alias": "r", "fields": {"A": {"column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, "N": {"column": "n", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "S": {"kind": "relation", "from": "s", "alias": "s", "fields": {"A": {"column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, "M": {"column": "m", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- mode
params
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> FILTER(_["N"] $== "zz") .> SORT_BY(_["N"]) .> TAKE(3) .> LINK(S, _1["A"] == _2["A"] AND _2["M"] $== "yy") .> MAP(RECORD("n", _["N"], "m", _["M"]))
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.`n` AS `n`, `s`.`m` AS `m` FROM (SELECT `r`.* FROM `r` `r` WHERE (CAST(`n` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin) ORDER BY CAST(`n` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin ASC LIMIT 3) `_sub1` INNER JOIN `s` `s` ON ((`_sub1`.`a` = `s`.`a`) AND (CAST(`s`.`m` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin))
--- params
t"zz", t"yy"
===

### name: stmt.derived.wrapped-columns-keep-a-fixed-order
--- note
GO-C18: the derived table a TAKE puts under a LINK lists the relation's columns,
and Go listed them in the order it happened to range over a map, so the same
program gave a different select list on different runs. The columns come out in
the binding's declaration order. The runner translates this case five times, and a
Go map order that changes between calls makes one of them differ.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"PRICE": {"column": "price", "type": "NUM"}, "QTY": {"column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}, "SKU": {"column": "sku", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "TAGS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "tags", "alias": "t", "fields": {"TAG": {"column": "tag", "type": "TEXT"}, "W": {"column": "w", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> TAKE(5) .> LINK(TAGS, a, b, a["NAME"] $== b["TAG"])
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.`price`, `_sub1`.`qty`, `_sub1`.`name`, `_sub1`.`sku`, `t`.`tag`, `t`.`w` FROM (SELECT `i`.* FROM `items` `i` LIMIT 5) `_sub1` INNER JOIN `tags` `t` ON (CAST(`_sub1`.`name` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin = CAST(`t`.`tag` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin)
===

### name: bind.column.statement-is-not-a-column-type
--- note
PY-C44: a column holds a NUM, TEXT, BOOL, BIN or UNKNOWN value (docs/internals/
sql-translation.md, kinds). STATEMENT and LIST are what a whole fragment can be;
a binding declared as one made `Fragment.kind` answer STATEMENT for a column.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "c", "type": "STATEMENT"}}
--- source
C
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

### name: bind.column.list-is-not-a-column-type
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "c", "type": "LIST"}}
--- source
C
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

### name: register.dialect.text-escape-must-escape-the-quote
--- note
JS-C25, PHP-C37, PY-C50, CPP-C53, LISP-C36: a textEscape of `{"\\": "\\\\"}` on a
MariaDB base escapes the backslash and nothing else, so `x' OR '1'='1` came out
as `'x' OR '1'='1'` inline. Decided: refused at registration.
--- dialect
my-nobs
--- register
[{"dialect": "my-nobs", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textEscape": {"\\": "\\\\"}}}]
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
S $== "x' OR '1'='1"
--- throws
LogicException
===

### name: register.dialect.empty-text-escape-is-refused
--- note
CPP-C53, LISP-C36, PHP-C37: an empty textEscape (or one dropped with null) on a
base that doubles quotes leaves every literal unescaped. R1.
--- dialect
sqlite-noesc
--- register
[{"dialect": "sqlite-noesc", "extends": "sqlite", "version": "3.48", "lexical": {"textEscape": {}}}]
--- source
"a' OR 1=1 --"
--- throws
LogicException
===

### name: register.dialect.escape-character-must-escape-itself
--- note
PHP-C37: `textEscape {"'": "\\'"}` on a MySQL-family base escapes the quote with a
backslash but does not touch a backslash in the input, so the input `\'` becomes
`\\'` -- an escaped backslash followed by a quote that terminates the literal.
R1: an escape character must itself be a key.
--- dialect
my-badbs
--- register
[{"dialect": "my-badbs", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textEscape": {"'": "\\'"}}}]
--- source
"a\\' OR 1=1 --"
--- throws
LogicException
===

### name: register.dialect.text-quote-must-differ-from-identifier-quote
--- note
PHP-C37, PY-C50: `textQuote: "` on ansi makes a text literal look like a quoted
identifier. R1: textQuote differs from identQuote.
--- dialect
dq
--- register
[{"dialect": "dq", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "lexical": {"textQuote": "\""}}]
--- source
"a\" OR 1=1 --"
--- throws
LogicException
===

% Rule R2 (program-supplied identifiers). An alias or column name that comes from a
% SEL TEXT literal -- a RECORD key, a SELECT_COLS name -- is held to the rules a
% binding's own names already meet: not empty, no NUL. The refusal is
% E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED at the literal (the SQL layer cannot spell it); binding
% construction keeps E_SQL_BINDING. On PostgreSQL an alias is also refused when
% it is longer than 63 bytes and another alias shares its 63-byte prefix (the
% server truncates and the two collide).

### name: alias.record-key-with-nul
--- note
PHP-C53, PY-C43, CPP-C58, GO-C37: `AS "a<NUL>b"` reached the statement on every dialect.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"PRICE": {"column": "price", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> MAP(RECORD("a\u{0}b", _["PRICE"]))
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:21
===

### name: alias.record-key-empty
--- note
PHP-C54, PY-C43, GO-C37: `AS ""` is refused by PostgreSQL and MariaDB.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"PRICE": {"column": "price", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> MAP(RECORD("", _["PRICE"]))
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:21
===

### name: alias.select-cols-name-with-nul
--- note
PY-C43, JS-C57: SELECT_COLS names on a relation with no declared fields bypass the allow-list and the NUL check.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SELECT_COLS("PRICE", "a\u{0}b")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:31
===

### name: alias.pg-truncated-aliases-collide
--- note
PHP-C54: PostgreSQL truncates an identifier to 63 bytes, so two 71-character keys
sharing their first 63 bytes name one column and SEL's two record keys become one.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"PRICE": {"column": "price", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> MAP(RECORD("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaX1", _["PRICE"], "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaX2", _["PRICE"]))
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:102
===

% Rule R3 (NUL in a text literal). A TEXT value containing U+0000 is refused by
% the SQL layer in every mode: PostgreSQL cannot store it, a C-string client API
% truncates the statement at it, and no dialect has a portable spelling. The
% refusal is E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED at the literal.

### name: lex.text.nul-is-refused-in-every-mode
--- note
GO-C43, PHP-C53, LISP-C41: the NUL byte was emitted raw in inline mode; params mode
hands it to the driver, which then decides. R3 refuses both.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
S $== "a\u{0}b"
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:7
===

% Rule R4 (raw fields are opaque). A binding declared `raw` is a SQL expression the
% application wrote against the relation's own alias. It survives FILTER, MAP and
% aggregates over the relation; anything that would have to NAME it as a column --
% SELECT_COLS, or a derived table a TAKE/SORT_BY/LIMIT wraps the relation in --
% is refused E_SQL_SHAPE at the reference.

### name: bind.raw-field.select-cols-refuses
--- note
JS-C28, CPP-C32: `SELECT i.total` for a raw field that has no column of that name.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "TOTAL": {"kind": "raw", "raw": "i.price * i.qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SELECT_COLS("TOTAL")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:22
===

### name: bind.raw-field.across-a-derived-table-refuses
--- note
JS-C28, CPP-C32: after SORT_BY .> TAKE the relation is a derived table that has no TOTAL column.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "alias": "i", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "TOTAL": {"kind": "raw", "raw": "i.price * i.qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SORT_BY(_["ID"]) .> TAKE(2) .> FILTER(_["TOTAL"] > 5)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:49
===

% Rule R5 (case-colliding names). SEL identifiers are upper-cased, so two binding
% names -- or two fields of one relation -- that differ only by ASCII case are the
% same name. Binding construction refuses them: E_SQL_BINDING.

### name: bind.relation.fields-differing-only-by-case
--- note
CPP-C55, PY-C44, PHP-C57: C++ kept the first, Python the last, PHP the last.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "t", "fields": {"A": {"column": "x", "type": "NUM"}, "a": {"column": "y", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ANY(R, I, I["A"] > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

### name: bind.names-differing-only-by-case
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"x": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, "X": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "b", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X > 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

% Rule R6 (correlate is an expression): the application's `correlate` is spliced
% into the aggregate templates parenthesised, so a top-level OR in it cannot change
% the meaning of the AND that follows. The default (no correlate: `lexical.true`)
% is not parenthesised.

### name: agg.correlate.is-parenthesised
--- note
CPP-C56: `oi.a=o.id OR oi.b=o.id AND (body)` binds the AND to the second operand only.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "oi", "alias": "oi", "correlate": {"raw": "oi.a=o.id OR oi.b=o.id"}, "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ANY(ITEMS, I, I["QTY"] > 0)
--- expect
EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `oi` `oi` WHERE (oi.a=o.id OR oi.b=o.id) AND ((`oi`.`qty` > 0)) IS TRUE)
===

### name: register.dialect.correct-pairing-is-accepted
--- note
CONTROL for the four refusals above: a registered escape map that doubles the
quote (and escapes the backslash, as a MySQL-family server needs) is accepted, and
translates to the same bytes every time.
--- dialect
my-ok
--- register
[{"dialect": "my-ok", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textEscape": {"\\": "\\\\", "'": "''"}}}]
--- source
"it's"
--- expect
'it''s'
===

### name: register.dialect.redefinition-with-the-same-parent-replaces
--- note
Re-registering a dialect name with the SAME `extends` replaces the earlier
registration: the second lexical override wins and nothing of the first remains.
--- dialect
redef
--- register
[{"dialect": "redef", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textCollate": " COLLATE utf8mb4_bin"}},
 {"dialect": "redef", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5", "lexical": {"textCollate": " COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin"}}]
--- source
"A" $== "a"
--- expect
(CAST('A' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin)
===

### name: register.dialect.redefinition-with-a-different-parent-is-refused
--- note
A name already registered under one parent cannot be re-pointed at another:
cases already translated through it would silently change meaning. A startup
mistake, so the host's own error type like every other refused registration.
--- dialect
redef2
--- register
[{"dialect": "redef2", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5"},
 {"dialect": "redef2", "extends": "postgresql", "version": "16"}]
--- source
1 + 1
--- throws
LogicException
===
